    <h1>Was the Union ever lawfully consented to?</h1>
    <p class="lede">Set out the sequence of events from March 1707 on their own, and the Union stops reading as a treaty two nations agreed to — and starts reading as a transfer of sovereignty completed before the one body entitled to approve it could ever meet. In other words, the "Union" was nothing other than the annexation of Scotland.</p>
    <div class="read-time">~7 min read</div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="case-body">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="mw-grid">
      <div>
        <div class="mw-point">
          <div class="mw-label">25 march 1707</div>
          <p>The Scottish Parliament adjourns, ordering members and "all others having interest" to reconvene on 22 April. This opened the door to the Scottish people and, crucially, the Convention of the Estates — in Scots constitutional practice, the only body that could extinguish or transfer the kingdom's sovereignty. The English set their trap.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mw-point">
          <div class="mw-label">the sitting that was prevented</div>
          <p>The target wasn't a re-vote. The Parliament's own members had already been managed — bribed, threatened, rewarded — and were never going to reverse themselves. What the adjournment actually opened was the clause "all others having interest": under Scots law, any person with standing could appear at a Convention and invoke <em>salvo jure cujuslibet</em>, the unrepealed safeguard preserving fundamental rights from statutory override. A single valid invocation could suspend the Union pending adjudication. With more than 20,000 names already on petitions against the Treaty, that sitting was never really about the men in the chamber. It was about everyone else.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mw-point">
          <div class="mw-label">28 april 1707</div>
          <p>Queen Anne dissolves the Scottish Parliament by a proclamation issued as though the Union it describes were already in force — even though, in law, Scotland remained a separate, sovereign kingdom for three more days. The English parliament remains, untouched by the union.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mw-point">
          <div class="mw-label">what's missing from the story</div>
          <p>The standard account of 1707 begins after this sequence and ends with the Union already made, which is exactly why the missing week is so rarely discussed: it's the one moment where refusal was still constitutionally possible.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mw-verdict">
          No adjourned sitting. No Convention of Estates. No tested consent. What that leaves isn't a treaty to renegotiate — it's an unratified transfer of power still being carried on custom and silence.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="record-card">
        <div class="rc-head">
          <span>primary source · 25 march 1707</span>
          <div class="rc-seal">A R</div>
        </div>
        <div class="rc-body">
          <blockquote>"…adjourns the same to 22 April next to come, ordaining all members of parliament… and all others having interest, to attend at Edinburgh that day."</blockquote>
          <div class="rc-cite">Records of the Parliaments of Scotland, NAS PA6/34</div>
          <div class="rc-explainer">The adjournment order itself — the clause that kept the door open for the Convention of Estates, and the clause that was never acted on.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mw-compare">
      <div class="mw-compare-head">
        <h3>Why "prevented" is the right word</h3>
        <p>The same constitutional right was exercised twice in this period, in opposite directions — and the same tool was used to decide the outcome both times.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="mw-compare-grid">
        <div class="mw-compare-col">
          <div class="mw-compare-year">1689</div>
          <h4>a competing army backs the Estates</h4>
          <p>When the Estates moved to remove a king, they didn't do it on courage alone. William's letter to the Estates that April confirms warships guarding the Scottish coast, regiments already reinforced within Scotland, and cavalry held in northern England on standing orders to intervene on the Estates' behalf.</p>
          <blockquote>"…if you have necessity."<cite>William III to the Estates, 2 April 1689 [1689/3/107]</cite></blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="mw-compare-col">
          <div class="mw-compare-year">1707</div>
          <h4>an army suppresses the Convention</h4>
          <p>Eighteen years on, the same right pointed the other way, and the same kind of force stopped it. The Duke of Marlborough's veterans — the most capable army in Europe — were positioned on and across the border, under instructions kept deliberately out of view.</p>
          <blockquote>"…all relating to this affaire must be kept very private."<cite>Sir David Nairne to the Earl of Mar, November 1706</cite></blockquote>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mw-compare-note">The constitutional principle didn't change between 1689 and 1707. Only which way the army was pointing did.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mw-layers">
      <div class="mw-layers-head">
        <h3>Three ways to make sure it never happened</h3>
        <p>Suppressing one clause of one adjournment order took more than a closed door — it worked on three independent levels at once, so that even defeating one still left the sitting impossible.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="mw-layers-grid">
        <div class="mw-layer">
          <div class="mw-layer-num">layer one — administrative</div>
          <h4>no writs, no meeting</h4>
          <p>No writs of summons were issued by the Clerk Register. Without them, no gathering anywhere carried constitutional standing — a meeting in a tavern on the Canongate would have had no more legal weight than any other private assembly.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mw-layer">
          <div class="mw-layer-num">layer two — military</div>
          <h4>the whole square, not one door</h4>
          <p>Contemporary accounts describe soldiers at the approaches to Parliament Close, sentries on the High Street and Canongate, and dragoons near the Netherbow Port — denial of the entire civic space in which any assembly could form.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mw-layer">
          <div class="mw-layer-num">layer three — constitutional</div>
          <h4>legitimacy lived in the writs</h4>
          <p>Even a gathering that evaded both of the above would have been powerless. A Convention's authority comes from the writs, not from whoever turns up. No writs, no Convention — however many people showed.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mw-layers-note">The silence that followed wasn't an accident. It was engineered, on three levels at once.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mw-compare">
      <div class="mw-compare-head">
        <h3>Wasn't the Alien Act just an opening threat?</h3>
        <p>It's sometimes argued that England's Alien Act of 1705 was withdrawn once serious negotiations began — proof that coercion was a one-off opening move, not a continuous condition. The primary record doesn't support that reading. The Act was one instrument in a wider coercive architecture that included active military preparation for invasion, sustained through to the vote.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="mw-point">
        <div class="mw-label">december 1704</div>
        <p>James Johnstone, Lord Register and a figure in close contact with English official opinion, wrote to Baillie of Jerviswood that opinion in England was running on "conquest or union." That framing — conquest as the explicit alternative — predates the Alien Act and sets the strategic context in which it was passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="mw-point">
        <div class="mw-label">1705 — the alien act</div>
        <p>The Act threatened to treat Scots resident in England as aliens and choke off Scottish trade unless a Union treaty was agreed by Christmas 1705. Even Daniel Defoe — hired by Robert Harley to sell the Union to Edinburgh — later admitted the Act had "in a manner declar'd open war with Scotland," calling it the most impolitic measure the English parliament had ever passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="mw-point">
        <div class="mw-label">november–december 1706</div>
        <p>As negotiations concluded, English troops were massed on the Scottish border and in Ulster — regiments of foot and horse on the border, a further foot and dragoon regiment across the water, with orders already given and secrecy insisted on in the private correspondence confirming it. Weeks later a further 800 horse marched to the border on Marlborough's direct instructions. These were operational deployments, not diplomatic gestures and it was unlikely that Scotland's poorly trained regiments could have resisted Marlborough's battle-hardened veterans.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="mw-verdict">
        The Alien Act set the coercive frame. The troop deployments maintained it, operationally, through to the vote. Withdrawing the Act didn't withdraw the army — as the legal scholar T.B. Smith concluded, Scotland's commissioners negotiated under the implied threat of invasion by one of history's great commanders, backed by one of Europe's most powerful states.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mw-compare">
      <div class="mw-compare-head">
        <h3>A defenceless negotiation</h3>
        <p>The imbalance went further than trained numbers. Scotland's own army wasn't available to defend Scottish sovereignty, because it wasn't under Scottish command.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="mw-point">
        <div class="mw-label">regiments abroad</div>
        <p>Scottish regiments — the Royal Scots, the Royal Scots Fusiliers, the Scots Greys — were serving under Marlborough on the continent, confirmed fighting at Ramillies in May 1706, six months before the correspondence documenting English troops massing on Scotland's own border.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="mw-point">
        <div class="mw-label">parliament already knew</div>
        <p>The Scottish Parliament had acknowledged this dependency itself in 1704, when it threatened to withdraw Scottish forces from the continental war as leverage against the Crown — confirming those forces answered to English-directed strategic command, not Scottish institutional authority.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="mw-verdict">
        Scotland faced the threat of invasion by Marlborough's veterans while its own trained soldiers were serving under Marlborough elsewhere. What was left to defend Scottish sovereignty was militia assessed as incapable of resistance — not simply a military imbalance, but the progressive absorption of Scottish military capacity into English command, leaving Scotland institutionally defenceless at the moment of maximum pressure.
      </div>
      <p class="mw-source-note">The full mechanism behind the suppression is set out in <a href="https://angrypict.substack.com/p/annexation-why-and-how" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Part II of the original analysis</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

